WEBVTT

NOTE
Generated from prototype/narration.md. Edit that file, not this one.

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Who else is already doing what you're about to build?

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.500
You type your idea the way you'd say it out loud.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.000
This one: an app for small farms selling straight to restaurants.

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.500
Your words aren't the market's words. It translates, then goes hunting.

5
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.500
It reads what each company publishes about itself.

6
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.500
Three competitors you'd never have found. You didn't need their names.

7
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:28.500
Prices first, copied from their own pricing pages. Quote them without checking.

8
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.500
One doesn't publish a price. So neither do we.

9
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.500
A blog quotes a price. Not from the company, so it sits outside the table, flagged, with a link.

10
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.500
Then how each one pitches itself, in their words rather than ours.

11
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.500
A question mark means we looked and found nothing. It never means no.

12
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.500
A triangle means a rival said it. Rivals aren't neutral.

13
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.500
No public updates? It says so, and lists every page it checked.

14
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.500
It reads the three-star reviews for what people wish existed. Six wanted per-restaurant pricing. Nobody sells it.

15
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.500
How busy each site is, and how long they've been at it.

16
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:08.500
Every figure names its source and its date. Rankings, not visitor guesses.

17
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:13.500
Every fact links to where it came from. The page, the sentence, the date.

18
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.500
That's a report you can hand to someone. The demo continues in the next film.
